  <!-- Main Editorial Content -->
  <article style="max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 0 16px 3rem; line-height: 1.8;">

    <!-- Introduction -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>Understanding the GMT to JST Conversion</h2>
      <p>
        Coordinating international corporate operations, global tech sprint reviews, gaming tournaments, livestock dispatches, and travel itineraries between Greenwich Mean Time (GMT) regions and Japan demands precise time management. When converting from <strong>Greenwich Mean Time (GMT)</strong> to <strong>Japan Standard Time (JST)</strong>, the foundational rule is that Japan operates 9 hours ahead of the Prime Meridian baseline.
      </p>
      <p>
        Greenwich Mean Time represents the zero-degree longitudinal reference point (UTC+0). Japan Standard Time is centered at 135° East longitude (passing through Akashi City in Hyogo Prefecture), positioning it in the ninth eastern time zone (UTC+9). Because Japan does not observe seasonal daylight saving time adjustments, this 9-hour gap remains constant 365 days a year.
      </p>
      <p>
        Whether you are scheduling video calls with partners in Tokyo, Osaka, or Kyoto, configuring server backup jobs, organizing esports streams, or booking flights to Narita (NRT) or Haneda (HND), using a date-aware converter ensures midnight calendar shifts are never missed.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- GMT to JST Time Difference -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>GMT to JST Time Difference</h2>
      <p>
        The precise time difference between Greenwich Mean Time and Japan Standard Time is <strong>9 hours</strong>. Japan Standard Time is permanently 9 hours ahead of Greenwich Mean Time.
      </p>
      <p>
        The conversion mathematical equation is expressed as:
      </p>
      <div style="background: var(--panel); border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: 12px; padding: 1.5rem; text-align: center; margin: 1.5rem 0;">
        <p style="font-size: 1.4rem; font-weight: 700; margin: 0; color: var(--accent);">JST = GMT + 9 Hours</p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.95rem; margin: 0.5rem 0 0; color: var(--text-muted);">UTC+0 (Prime Meridian) → UTC+9 (Japan Standard Time)</p>
      </div>
      <p>
        Because the offset is positive (+9), locations in Japan experience daylight and calendar dates hours before Greenwich observatory coordinates do. When London or Greenwich marks 09:00 AM GMT in the morning, Tokyo has already reached 18:00 (6:00 PM) JST in the early evening.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Image 1: Time Difference Infographic -->
    <div style="max-width: 750px; margin: 0 auto 2.5rem;">
      <img
        src="/assets/gmt-to-jst-time-difference.webp"
        alt="GMT to JST time difference showing a nine hour offset"
        width="1200" height="800"
        loading="eager"
        style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--line);"
      />
    </div>

    <!-- How to Convert GMT to JST -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>How to Convert GMT to JST</h2>
      <p>
        Converting Greenwich Mean Time to Japan Standard Time manually is straightforward when following a 7-step procedure:
      </p>
      <ol style="padding-left: 1.2rem; line-height: 1.9;">
        <li><strong>Start with the GMT date and time:</strong> Identify the clock time in GMT (e.g., 14:30 GMT).</li>
        <li><strong>Verify the GMT baseline:</strong> Confirm that the baseline uses UTC+0 without daylight saving modifications.</li>
        <li><strong>Identify JST's offset:</strong> Note that Japan Standard Time uses UTC+9 year-round.</li>
        <li><strong>Add nine hours:</strong> Sum the GMT hour figure with 9 (14:30 + 9 hours = 23:30).</li>
        <li><strong>Check whether the result passes midnight:</strong> If your calculated hour reaches or exceeds 24:00, subtract 24 hours from the total.</li>
        <li><strong>Adjust the calendar date if needed:</strong> If the result rolled over 24:00, advance the calendar day, month, or year by one.</li>
        <li><strong>Confirm the final JST time:</strong> Record the resulting hour, minute, date, and JST timezone label (e.g., 23:30 JST).</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        For instance, converting <strong>14:30 GMT</strong> yields <strong>23:30 JST</strong> (11:30 PM JST) on the exact same date. However, converting <strong>16:30 GMT</strong> yields 16:30 + 9 = 25:30. Subtracting 24 hours results in <strong>01:30 JST (1:30 AM JST) on the next calendar day</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- 24-Hour Conversion Table -->
    <section class="content-section" style="margin-bottom: 2.5rem;">
      <h2>GMT to JST 24-Hour Conversion Table</h2>
      <p>
        The standard reference table below provides a complete hour-by-hour map converting Greenwich Mean Time (00:00 through 23:00) to Japan Standard Time. Rows highlighted in colored styling indicate times where adding 9 hours advances the clock into the <strong>next calendar day</strong> in Japan.
      </p>
      <div style="overflow-x: auto; border-radius: 12px; border: 1px solid var(--line);">
        <table style="width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 0.95rem;">
          <thead>
            <tr style="background: var(--panel);">
              <th style="padding: 12px 16px; text-align: left; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">GMT (12-Hour)</th>
              <th style="padding: 12px 16px; text-align: left; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">GMT (24-Hour)</th>
              <th style="padding: 12px 16px; text-align: left; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">JST (12-Hour)</th>
              <th style="padding: 12px 16px; text-align: left; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">JST (24-Hour)</th>
              <th style="padding: 12px 16px; text-align: left; border-bottom: 2px solid var(--line);">Calendar Status</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            {conversionRows.map((row, i) => (
              <tr style={`background: ${row.dayNote === "Next day" ? "rgba(var(--accent-rgb, 99, 102, 241), 0.12)" : i % 2 === 0 ? "transparent" : "var(--panel)"}`}>
                <td style="padding: 10px 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line);">{row.gmt}</td>
                <td style="padding: 10px 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); font-family: monospace;">{row.gmt24}</td>
                <td style="padding: 10px 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); font-weight: 600; color: var(--accent);">{row.jst}</td>
                <td style="padding: 10px 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); font-family: monospace; font-weight: 600;">{row.jst24}</td>
                <td style="padding: 10px 16px; border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line); font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--text-muted);">{row.dayNote}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p style="font-size: 0.88rem; color: var(--text-muted); margin-top: 0.75rem;">
        Note: Starting from 15:00 GMT (3:00 PM GMT), every subsequent GMT hour translates to the next calendar date in Japan.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Conversion Examples -->
